<h2>How the dispute reached the appellate court</h2>
<p>The book, published in December 2020, chronicles the rise of Yes Bank&rsquo;s co-founder and the crisis that engulfed the bank. Its subject sued for defamation before the District Judge at Tis Hazari, targeting the book along with two online articles and an interview, pleading injury to reputation, privacy and personality rights and asserting potential prejudice to his pending criminal proceedings. By a common order of 10 July 2024, the trial court granted an interim injunction under Order XXXIX Rules 1 and 2 CPC: sale and circulation of the book were restrained, and the online material was ordered taken down. The publisher appealed under Order XLIII Rule 1(r) read with Section 104 CPC; the author — who had been proceeded ex parte below — filed a connected appeal.</p>

<h2>The appellate lens: review, not re-trial</h2>
<p>The judgment opens where every injunction appeal must: the standard of interference. Following the settled line beginning with <em>Wander Ltd. v. Antox India</em>, the appellate court does not ask whether it would have granted the injunction; it asks whether the trial court&rsquo;s discretion miscarried — through arbitrariness, perversity, or disregard of settled principles. Within that frame, the court worked through the record. The trial court had examined sixteen statements and found only statements (iv), (vii) and (x) prima facie defamatory — passages imputing use of personal influence in a hiring, disparaging the subject&rsquo;s intellectual capacity, and alleging specific professional impropriety in credit approvals — and statement (ii), an &ldquo;arrogant, erstwhile tycoon&rdquo; passage, derogatory though not defamatory. The plaintiff did not challenge the findings clearing the other twelve. The appellate battlefield, the court held, was therefore confined to those four.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Statements (iv), (vii), (x)</h3><p>The High Court found no infirmity in the prima facie findings: imputations of fact presented without established foundation are not rescued by being framed as comment, and hearsay or pending investigations do not prove truth at the interim stage.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Statement (ii)</h3><p>Characterised below as derogatory rather than defamatory, it could not independently support an injunction; its publication was kept in abeyance pending the trial court&rsquo;s final determination.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The other twelve</h3><p>Unchallenged findings in the appellants&rsquo; favour — including the finding that no prejudice to the criminal trial was shown — stood, and defined how much of the book could lawfully be restrained.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The operative mismatch</h3><p>An analysis that condemned four passages had produced an order silencing the entire work. That disproportion is what the appeal corrected: the blanket restraint was lifted and the book permitted to circulate without the offending passages.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The free speech calculus</h2>
<p>Two strands of doctrine met in this case. On one side, the heightened caution that governs pre-trial restraints on journalistic and literary work: following the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent reiteration in the <em>Bloomberg</em> matter, the three-fold test of prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable injury cannot be applied mechanically to gag publications before trial, and courts must record detailed reasons on each element. On the other, the court&rsquo;s equally firm reminder that public-figure status and prior circulation do not extinguish the right to reputation, and that a defamatory passage gains no immunity from its setting within a larger literary work. The result is a calibrated order rather than a victory for either absolutism: the book speaks, minus the four passages whose prima facie assessment survived appellate scrutiny, and the final word on all of them awaits trial.</p>

<h2>Lessons for litigants on both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Match the relief to the finding.</strong> Plaintiffs who press for blanket restraints beyond what the court&rsquo;s own analysis supports hand the defendant a ready appellate ground; severable relief is durable relief.</li>
  <li><strong>Challenge what hurts, concede what does not.</strong> The plaintiff&rsquo;s decision not to appeal the findings on twelve statements shrank the appellate dispute decisively. Appeal strategy is as much about what is left alone as what is attacked.</li>
  <li><strong>Interim orders are built on reasons.</strong> After the recent Supreme Court jurisprudence, an injunction against publication that does not reason through all three elements is appellate-vulnerable; defendants should scrutinise the order&rsquo;s architecture, not only its conclusion.</li>
  <li><strong>Ex parte status has a price.</strong> The author&rsquo;s absence below meant the record was built without him. Parties served in defamation suits over books and articles should appear early — the interim stage is frequently the whole war.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Publishers and authors facing injunction applications should prepare a severability map of the challenged work at the outset — statement by statement, with the factual basis for each. It equips the court to tailor relief, and if a broad restraint nonetheless issues, the same map becomes the backbone of the FAO. Claimants, conversely, should plead innuendo and reputational harm with particulars for each passage; findings won on specifics survive appeal, while omnibus grievances invite omnibus reversal.</p>
</div>
